course: dictionary
lesson: 6407
title: Mush: soft, sled or soppy?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can mean very different things in different places. Context, the words around it, tells you which meaning the writer wants. «Leafcutter ants do not eat the leaves; they chew them into mush to grow a fungus garden.» Chewing leaves and growing a fungus garden are the clues, so mush here is a soft, squashed mass.
explain: Always test your meaning. Put it in the sentence instead of the hard word: «they chew them into a soft, squashed mass to grow a fungus garden» still makes sense, so you are right.
explain: The same word can have other meanings. "Mush!" shouted at sled dogs in the snow means "go faster!", and "Oi, mush!" said to a man in the UK is a rough way to call him. Look at where the word is and who is speaking.
explain: Phrases work differently too. "My brain is mush" does not mean your brain is squashed food. It means you feel very tired and muddled. When context still leaves you unsure, check a dictionary.
fact: Mush (noun) means a soft, wet, squashed mass, like overcooked food.
fact: Mush can also mean silly, soppy talk or writing about love.
fact: "Mush!" (interjection) tells sled dogs to start or go faster; to mush (verb) means to travel with a dog sled.
fact: Old-fashioned UK slang: mush can mean a man, like "mate", or a person's face or mouth, as in "Shut your mush".
fact: "Turn to mush" means become soft and squashed, and "my brain is mush" means you feel tired and muddled.
exercise: «Leafcutter ants ... chew them into mush» What does mush mean here? | A sharp, hard stone | A loud shout | A kind of song | *A soft, squashed mass
exercise: Dogs race across the snow when someone shouts "Mush!" What does the word tell the dogs to do? | Stop and rest | Come inside | Lie down | *Start running or go faster
exercise: Ella says, "My brain is mush after that test." What does she mean? | She has a sore mouth | *She feels tired and muddled | She is cooking porridge | Her brain is squashed flat
test: "Oi, mush!" said to a man in the UK means what? | Silly love talk | *A rough way to call a man, like "mate" | Soft, wet food | Start the sled
test: When used as a verb about sleds, what does "to mush" mean? | To cook soft food | To sing softly | To stop suddenly | *To travel with a dog sled
exam: In «they chew them into mush», which clue shows that mush is something made from food? | The word ants | The word not | The word farming | *The word chew, which shows the leaves are being broken up
